#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165abac8-b78d-5d8b-9797-98764bec8a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459473} {:answer/id #uuid "9682a02a-0b80-592c-b204-2e00e64c9de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459473} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8082197-4ec5-5193-873e-90d47f00746b" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460603} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e01fbca2-a092-5b1e-95d7-018e7d1f5edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459475} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd8e7a9-c801-5eeb-bd2b-c483bdb34b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459476}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459476} :answer-field/id #uuid "422cd737-9b8c-53f2-a0cd-c12e1ad7286c" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fbcc0ff-2235-56bb-aa08-904c6e3a229b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459477} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c4988b-246a-5c24-99ad-02a2f31d8f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459478} :answer-field/id #uuid "07711a4e-3e3f-5c90-b74e-ec28eded9fbf" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "58d69dfd-31b1-5851-b7cd-9ea022ec672e" :question/math-academy-id "q-125380" :question/problem "![](images/14/145b9dcc1e52dec65d64245afa89fba77e0d0cda5a919b52009e2397f3f03e16.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/20/20121d30445c9e756f1c2c024713bc6cd6f0464cfcfc24df68bc30c766c4988e.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/0f/0ff98536c275458d68deabda91f56bf63e7c8f59f5ea79c68292332d8cd58d4f.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47df525a-0d27-5474-842e-e797864d924f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459479} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4b12aa-dfc8-5cf6-bb46-fa30cda83363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459479} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ebe448c-6d78-52eb-b87a-c0f6378a526d" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460606} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b3a5163-4335-5da1-a308-4afc6fa83343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459481} {:answer/id #uuid "2865bf65-f4fa-5527-9338-dac5b94be08d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459482} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a6785c8-4fa8-5419-98ef-b6ca891fd2fe" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7f457e-5e87-5921-9c97-354280faa2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459483} {:answer/id #uuid "58d82791-fb0b-511f-a411-3a2ed07c51ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459484} :answer-field/id #uuid "5480c90b-6e84-5672-a102-83d8323aaae1" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "26c0cce0-6deb-59c6-a9c4-06e4698080d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-125381" :question/problem "![](images/50/501a3512fb4b8a1898c6dfb8816d587de4a0cd585991d9c92d2bb35d83a2853d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c11b38f9e598e0cd8fc50715d2091e5bea48d188440ae8a99c84d66a6acb6b9.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/34/346007ae582fccdf4519a3d1cd50a467e19d5345238343a1311fd3348bbeb138.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28492e5f-19b4-5757-9d39-0a839e1cd010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459497} {:answer/id #uuid "28528a48-293d-52d1-843c-d4ff315aef34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459498}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459497} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f5c61b9-3843-5dd3-92b0-873626260a5d" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460615} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f54574f-6fd8-5eb5-9310-72c1143f6efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459499} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d6bd3f-6edc-52da-9997-bbd134508244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459500}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459500} :answer-field/id #uuid "668c3e19-0580-5d17-90d1-02011568ce2a" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460616} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6aa09ec-ff05-50f8-950f-485b72db9ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459501} {:answer/id #uuid "79e22fbd-6db9-5639-8366-60b6e8a7bc2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459502} :answer-field/id #uuid "25b845de-7f7f-521c-bf75-76f02fd4110e" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "678d2be6-52f5-5dc7-bd50-f78db318a713" :question/math-academy-id "q-125410" :question/problem "![](images/82/82e3ae63881637f258ea92238bbcfc70f2e0993c17d74ce950d6457ffcd95039.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/d3/d363cf00c4901fa0c29bb9521cdd2ea3c12c377dd8e38306df0d5a2837915a36.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c5799e631e7eb45ec5a9711ef1569d51b376d8c764a3226caa2676cdb968a6f5.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0150bf9f-bc52-5d7c-baa1-278be0ec41d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}∇(x+y+z)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536288} {:answer/id #uuid "754a17e4-04df-525d-9c6f-927705d19c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536478} {:answer/id #uuid "e4847fce-4e49-589a-b6ee-7837047bacf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536640} {:answer/id #uuid "f52c058f-37bd-5f7c-a31f-2c35690a82a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∇(x+y+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536676} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7ad197-686c-54ac-8451-0dbb07ea69a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536691}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536640} :answer-field/id #uuid "215217ab-57d4-55ca-a3b0-79e58a5c7e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f08d86dc-4f87-54db-ae1a-88ae0082bc5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-125422" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}z\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+R\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P=z,Q=x,R=y.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}z\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $F=z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f54ef5-437d-5af8-a751-b33cd1dcdd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459517} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca4da76-b626-5282-9e3e-c1842c95aac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459518}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459518} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ea5b52-19ab-545d-b803-91faf4690e76" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460625} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a9ad46d-5411-5071-a050-a7cba8cd0ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459519} {:answer/id #uuid "e615d0a2-c730-5dba-9263-c8a081802791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459519} :answer-field/id #uuid "743e0df3-174a-5257-a160-51980afa07d4" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460626} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021ca85e-dc58-5e81-8883-f8dafe828184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459521} {:answer/id #uuid "2081d764-18e3-59ca-9a1b-b5feb261819b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459522} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b26c2a4-4801-5c19-84fb-9b2aa432ffa6" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2d693ae-0e11-561b-9b69-4ee74bfcc078" :question/math-academy-id "q-126726" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e679efaa2612d45bbfe8b918de7148853b64eaf530af34a525ef1735e0946b09.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is true, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fb/fbcd6fd0fadcc955212a145de9edb4654ddb92ddebb8ba31eb775cff676efeeb.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/a3/a3d398d382e6bc94ddde77e650d50a6b5ec050083f89f64104f6f2fd172a9239.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive”; “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e48c47-8ee2-519f-87f2-b570d3b8dba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459523} {:answer/id #uuid "cc34336b-2e10-5376-83aa-f53156f87b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459523} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e423910-3dd4-5ab2-a129-1141a47b6bb0" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460628} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bc0bec0-62de-5062-ba34-89678d7a79ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459525} {:answer/id #uuid "970b48b7-8b9c-59fe-a14e-26cd96ab46a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459526}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459526} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea2eff81-f7dd-5d77-8c8f-1eb3b01feb94" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e835fdd-dbbb-5253-a963-0bad2097d16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459527} {:answer/id #uuid "2d95a79c-9182-55b2-8789-e4ef4cdec237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459527} :answer-field/id #uuid "755d3cce-0252-5c3d-be63-5bdb7c00b632" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "505ec5b2-1b42-5150-bddf-6e7219956cd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126728" :question/problem "![](images/77/772added7ce46ba0ec25de66dfd03e0107787d851006f261ac8843eba008a2d2.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is false, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65323ed0-7186-569d-b96d-d952e703e09f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$F$ has no stream function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177872} {:answer/id #uuid "03c4eae9-5741-51cc-ae04-ffcd3dbf9ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489523} {:answer/id #uuid "1c26d151-cb3a-594e-8720-a6c407ef320d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489580} {:answer/id #uuid "cbeedaa1-96af-5558-afe2-43dff6b5725b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489964} {:answer/id #uuid "f53b17eb-0f4e-5b3d-ad45-ea23e6a6bb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490045}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490045} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4b1a0f0-52c7-552f-8f30-6e87f40ec522" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "60134b61-53f9-50e7-85c6-4445150f503c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126829" :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-2x)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2xy.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68b2771c-3bf6-5560-b718-7272a58b3b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260790} {:answer/id #uuid "736d4540-14f3-51e5-83fc-72912b452936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260884} {:answer/id #uuid "a638c5e9-524c-5a5b-8853-188f7d08eba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261321} {:answer/id #uuid "bb564d9a-1d00-56fb-a8bb-81270d654610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261524} {:answer/id #uuid "c00b58a6-3000-5270-ae8d-919718716fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261572}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261572} :answer-field/id #uuid "9380c67a-4a39-5f09-adf3-46f93da5f70a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ba1404b-6716-5e6e-975f-e1670e68a51c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126833" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=x\\,i+y\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/38/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (xi+y\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}-{y}^{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0256a8e6-ff43-5ac8-af7e-6b20461a1f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259891} {:answer/id #uuid "31a4a27e-adae-5b71-a64f-340e4d163c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260321} {:answer/id #uuid "458c9e50-9ca7-5a83-a136-566c24bdb806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260481} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef68d7c-1da2-5bcb-9618-02f37f9bc30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260975} {:answer/id #uuid "c93f48b0-cdb7-5f6f-b78d-d76054d5d2fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260481} :answer-field/id #uuid "8418b50e-8c27-58c7-87d6-b5b2519f456a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ee07bcc4-1a59-560b-bb9b-97cdb795faec" :question/math-academy-id "q-126850" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b37c10064003374c146c58bba99ff55304529ee66c9fb38c44246ccc90e8f402.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4c4f2f3d5b6188ac5a6ec41bb96fc0c7132cc39308cebfa16ee759eabac2c75.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320053d8-7061-5bee-a125-4b5572fa81bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260325} {:answer/id #uuid "7754550d-7915-5725-8462-298bbf3140d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260917} {:answer/id #uuid "89563c80-e409-593a-b874-aab987bd64d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261072} {:answer/id #uuid "906a6921-6dc7-5a65-9058-0fd796a13a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261132} {:answer/id #uuid "cd431924-213d-5018-895a-46685548488d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261132} :answer-field/id #uuid "001f66bd-2162-519e-af79-734f33a25866" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea876586-e09c-5050-847d-bc8b312ea302" :question/math-academy-id "q-126881" :question/problem "![](images/19/19fca0188077e56d6b56048d1cd48ea840922cafe7873aaa02937c8c2845e998.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=2\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180037 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c270d16-d1b9-584c-a753-2613c93b2733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177892} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ecac2-757d-5844-815f-910c23411410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177893} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2cb1ed-8729-57d0-bfa3-5b2591155680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177894} {:answer/id #uuid "0185a290-822d-58a0-ae4e-dfba4ef2a94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177895} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7388ca-c74f-5074-90cf-b3f8cb2908d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177896} :answer-field/id #uuid "15b24aa2-07c6-5f2c-85f4-99a1708ccb2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e3c5efb-b46d-57f5-8660-cecbe88198e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126892" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4e47604540f3cfb3f73af34b0d3eb64bb649e9a42004d22eccc2dd9007de4fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2x\\,i+y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=16.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(2x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 16+2\\cdot 8=48.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc34a4ce-679b-5695-9dfe-540cd60117ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177922} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fed570-9a0a-5fb6-b092-18090d3757e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177923} {:answer/id #uuid "034cd8e5-64a2-50b6-bec2-58e1146cb7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177924} {:answer/id #uuid "64ad22d1-d74c-5598-8e39-3026099a604c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177925} {:answer/id #uuid "73d700d6-ddb9-5085-a028-fa38b1441baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177925} :answer-field/id #uuid "268bf099-9758-5e4a-bc30-751c0618a551" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30107d9c-6bc8-5ab5-a34a-0024b1e426f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127199" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c8aef7238da3bb5aed0fd2a458f6fd9bcd42884bbd350616e58e7a25b1f0fe2.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/87/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-2x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6(3-(-3)) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-36)=-144.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0b425eb-22e7-5126-9c59-1b5123683fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177928} {:answer/id #uuid "12c2cb4f-61be-53d6-a2c3-6683264b610e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177929} {:answer/id #uuid "302cc11d-b1c7-5509-a279-663acfb5cc82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177930} {:answer/id #uuid "307bb29b-0e2c-55ac-acbb-7cf8129ef5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177931} {:answer/id #uuid "3254ddc0-ccb7-5590-9971-d8a69bbb023a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177928} :answer-field/id #uuid "f52107b4-b94f-5cca-902b-99cbc154821a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99c9a666-1234-5907-897c-c576caf3c171" :question/math-academy-id "q-127205" :question/problem "![](images/96/9610c7c482b561f4893b562bb6cf891136b30bea20c73fd56caa9a1a12a7a088.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-3x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00a9df76d0be8ffd33b2ee968b57a21eb1e6947af6e94505313f163b584f5769.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=-12.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-12)+2\\cdot (-8)=-40.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7201bd4-fc6d-593c-b256-cd883584c07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x, y) = (2\\sin y - e^{y})i + 2\\sin yj" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178168} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd0ed7b-756d-544a-8ce7-29c9d0b67c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x, y) = (2x\\cos y - e^{y})i - 2\\sin yj" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178169} {:answer/id #uuid "e274ed41-a0e3-5272-b7a5-4fb7823fcc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x, y) = 2x\\sin yi - 2\\cos yj" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178170} {:answer/id #uuid "49dec924-5e5a-5917-b267-04c2672831f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x, y) = 2x\\cos yi + (2\\sin y - e^{y})j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178171} {:answer/id #uuid "778c9c99-579c-5fd1-8f2d-44c844c975f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x, y) = 2\\cos yi - 2\\sin yj" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178172}] :answer-field/id #uuid "60de2cec-915a-5fb8-8f2a-f569f553631a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178167}] :question/id #uuid "657057fd-1eef-54ad-ad59-927f14130de9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161434" :question/problem "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x, y) = 2x\\sin y - e^{y}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2ec7aa8-dd39-59f0-9c72-cd2b71897203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477159} {:answer/id #uuid "ecba3797-1b79-59e1-94cf-45937dc83664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477160} :answer-field/id #uuid "e41a182b-f440-53a0-a770-6b1b1f8ee187" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477549} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3b05c34-4850-52cc-b19c-672abb4a9748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477161} {:answer/id #uuid "073a9b12-3569-542b-90dc-67ecc19682c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477161} :answer-field/id #uuid "8255f2e6-c5ca-5d62-a67a-bf22d30dd31f" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477550} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66a622b6-7c56-5c1d-8127-475db45665ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477163} {:answer/id #uuid "38009be4-58e5-583a-827a-bf14ac36798b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477163} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47f7fc9-bc5c-5be1-9460-c233b25d60e2" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)=2x\\,i-y\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3be31acc-b938-5628-8e53-a18ffa022892" :question/math-academy-id "q-161436" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0+0=0\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y+2y\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\,i-y\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+(-1)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "953fd242-8181-5605-91fe-317fa11da825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$F$ has no stream function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178180} {:answer/id #uuid "1060863a-42e6-5905-845d-98d31c4eb37b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=-4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489552} {:answer/id #uuid "20e8f93c-cee8-5e58-b256-bc513601b9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=y-x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489590} {:answer/id #uuid "29b072d1-5990-5d33-8af7-5bbaca56e59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=1-x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489608} {:answer/id #uuid "430fd24f-3207-570e-9148-bac816a3c0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186178180} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2f1e142-6aa3-5c72-9320-64f30d6f7c91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4bf80022-982e-52f6-a693-b1484ab92175" :question/math-academy-id "q-161437" :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=(1-2xy)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=(1-2xy)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=1-2xy$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=-2y+(-2y)=-4y\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6a58d74-7a8e-5cd4-92be-e6fb2ddca955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/62/62c887e92e6fa92a8a18e4836cad0762e163937417f24f90213ffcbd26325887.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178198} {:answer/id #uuid "b4eeeb03-a0ac-59c3-854b-378278da3d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/bf/bf0f4b592cde648f63b21523aba1a49aeeb3b4dcaf909ba72c4629e088ca372a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178199} {:answer/id #uuid "e4da5435-9993-5d07-b685-56a4bbc78975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6b/6b425704394d6fcd3090b54a3767b031b728cf4dbc44f9ac9446defe1cdb05aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178200} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc448a7-1afa-5f34-bf5f-3a0696758597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c1/c1b841b82f1c84ba4beb4dcda67cca017317c0666d61415477e23f7fa9d57e90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178201} {:answer/id #uuid "55788896-3612-5727-a2d7-360f8f02b37b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/67/67e9cfee7779fdde389a0804e7f52372ca9434de56b6611d296d4ac9c0759f72.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186178198} :answer-field/id #uuid "88b52b67-5ba9-5063-a5dd-28ae9e523684" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6aea8ef6-21e0-5151-a6ef-c9ce05078e1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162148" :question/problem "![](images/55/55957095fc97f1a5b56f7bed1c6715c1df5fc2c7aee707b2c2e4d7ed34d9dce8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following shows the streamlines of this vector field?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eac7ad4-820f-5c18-9773-ebf398898a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372015} {:answer/id #uuid "282750a9-b937-50f2-972a-4e331bd8e692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372016} {:answer/id #uuid "373316d8-46cd-53f6-9b91-7cb17ade342b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372017} {:answer/id #uuid "27a69ac4-2f12-5bf5-8488-d73f81f4be67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372018} {:answer/id #uuid "4883ebfa-3093-54d8-a60b-84cb2521bc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372019}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372017} :answer-field/id #uuid "5729d00f-ef28-5624-a8a5-015c4a539268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eca1e58e-6616-5dd0-8afa-579d4de9a32a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162209" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the streamline that passes through the point $P(-1,-1)$ for the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n. \n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-2x)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2xy.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curve of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ that passes through $P.$\nFirst, we find the value of the stream function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(-1,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the streamline that passes through $P(-1,-1)$ is the $-1$-level curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the streamline that passes through $P(-1,-1)$ is $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\nThe vector field and the streamline through the point $P$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b803554d852d985c8feca61804a9e52423391fc4624a01c4199d0744fbf2e28d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c07575d-294c-5293-b24e-6a9417e0599e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}(2+yx)\\,i-({y}^{3}{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489579} {:answer/id #uuid "1c3baa68-a9a3-512e-ac2c-44e118883d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}(2+yx)\\,i-(yx{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489581} {:answer/id #uuid "20503e7d-e6bc-5f3b-867f-11160df1589b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=yx{e}^{xy}(2+yx)\\,i-({y}^{2}x{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489589} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4b5532-5cfc-569d-94b2-251c025302d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}(2+y)\\,i-({y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489656} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a03243-e451-5382-82f5-0ae156239adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}(2+y)\\,i-({y}^{3}{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489579} :answer-field/id #uuid "c322713a-088c-53bf-ac8d-8ce18e48102c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b583ac2-a10b-5624-9dd5-8092730bcd5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162514" :question/problem "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y{e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}x{e}^{xy} \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}(2+yx), \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-({y}^{3}{e}^{xy}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}(2+yx)\\,i-({y}^{3}{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8719c4c1-296a-5308-8304-35f3d974ce70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178228} {:answer/id #uuid "90a5767b-02fa-5b05-a4c9-973f6b09f516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178229} {:answer/id #uuid "996baf61-e5f6-5df1-aa32-1851db148914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178230} {:answer/id #uuid "ff342f1d-0cbd-5406-9161-19ff3ff1afe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178231} {:answer/id #uuid "7776b280-63fe-5dc8-85a0-f54e021ddc7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186178230} :answer-field/id #uuid "712e9e01-c44e-5384-90fb-ae7811625631" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58115dca-1aaa-5449-a87f-7951d165abcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162640" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=4xy\\,i+{y}^{4}\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,i+3y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)={e}^{xy}\\,i-y{e}^{x}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=4y+4{y}^{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y{e}^{xy}+(-{e}^{x})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06952f7f-4bfc-548a-858e-98687ea45c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324032} {:answer/id #uuid "5f81d6d6-6127-530e-ac7f-c4e213abe7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324769} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f6ee80-a912-57f5-a285-2e0f6930700b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325354} {:answer/id #uuid "aa85942c-1e5b-56e8-8e97-ecf540eb889c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325422} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a60610-258b-5f05-a158-e2b14597409d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324769} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a311ac2-5c19-587c-a392-8c71de68fc0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eca1e45a-1d3f-5ae4-a5ee-83a793b7f6b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39342" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2-t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t)\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=2-t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2-t+t)\\,i+(2-t-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) \\\\  & =-2+(2-2t) \\\\  & =-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e57c7d-ef79-5439-be59-40bbdc9b3e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415412} {:answer/id #uuid "7cadedbb-d8e2-5bdf-97a6-9ad1eb1ceece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415420} {:answer/id #uuid "c58f6ab5-55eb-56af-a44a-0c359a236b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415424} {:answer/id #uuid "dc50983f-8d67-57c5-a859-8dc7f564511a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415427} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c20e31-578e-5df9-8b7f-fd828cbb1b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415429} :answer-field/id #uuid "55008b23-7b99-5d0d-87f9-a75bcea2d1c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "93a8c36b-e0d3-5d9c-9527-8161a7655e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-39344" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,4),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\cdot 1+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}t+2{t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =2+8 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad37157-9a52-53de-8a88-f35bbd20faae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324312} {:answer/id #uuid "41eaf9b6-c62c-5a18-b922-6e8c121cab26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324503} {:answer/id #uuid "66de0861-eaa6-53b8-b659-704734a18a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324831} {:answer/id #uuid "8772142e-f3d2-5968-9d7e-fa44127516d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325124} {:answer/id #uuid "bc84b5b2-8cab-547c-8cee-e174ba0ddddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324503} :answer-field/id #uuid "813be4d0-fa70-524e-afa9-65eb377f0d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3e395277-17c1-5003-9ac0-10817fce6c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-39674" :question/problem "![](images/51/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\n${C}_{1}$ is the section of parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =t+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =[2+8]-2 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1c650e8-11a0-52eb-872a-b80e91ee30fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178607} {:answer/id #uuid "d35a1bfb-6883-5741-a942-a7daa5cf9a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178608} {:answer/id #uuid "05cb2099-ecaa-5c5f-9f08-bbfc784a05c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178609} {:answer/id #uuid "1b0ccd47-0269-52a7-b9f5-d64377f51a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178610} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbf8a9d-e82a-5376-8c65-f152b09d8847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178611}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d8e4dc0d-67c6-574f-9ac0-06e759852449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178606}] :question/id #uuid "48c3ffb7-0c2f-51cd-baed-2f769ffcd563" :question/math-academy-id "q-39954" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = 2xy$ along the line segment from $(1, 2)$ to $(2, 1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84a68544-3dee-56d2-b4c7-eeed22d1e2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179213} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9036a3-920f-5615-bfeb-f807ba857652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179214} {:answer/id #uuid "d94aea65-e2a6-5fe5-abd3-22496a932674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179215} {:answer/id #uuid "15c8a75a-7643-52b0-b284-d4374f9fef41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179216} {:answer/id #uuid "173fb852-8a5c-5d4b-acb1-8126fdbffabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179217}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6f151015-d188-52ae-99fa-d6c0a2bc7f89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179212}] :question/id #uuid "bd6e5c6e-c45a-513e-b007-721402a77203" :question/math-academy-id "q-76200" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)\\sin \\left(\\frac{πy}{4}\\right)ds\n$$ along the line segment from $(1, 0, 1)$ to $(2, 2, 3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "092e02f4-a48a-5e04-9e64-41122eafc4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342314} {:answer/id #uuid "32123023-9c69-58b4-ab02-b4da1d9fe637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342661} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee53d4c-ce0e-5c65-b50c-2b82aac4e434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342759} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3356df-04ac-5b19-8ede-9a2b3f13406b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343316} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fb15c7-bb0e-5a45-9c69-b48603c7d63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344137}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343316} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ad2dbc-8ee7-590b-b513-269ed66bd87a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9aa3be4f-70c9-57d5-8b81-21b143de9479" :question/math-academy-id "q-76473" :question/problem "![](images/10/103c586778cdd8181fdc8396a92472df0525fc8e1c77fde9a7c3f731dca6df07.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $OABO,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ is the same path as ${C}_{2}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1409abbb-7224-58eb-8da4-c50ec502212f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342414} {:answer/id #uuid "18044e07-611f-56ee-ac44-f721c8ac319d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342450} {:answer/id #uuid "5caf092f-4769-58a7-b091-5b21981adeeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343016} {:answer/id #uuid "6826a076-9cd9-5d33-b76d-0b263e9a96dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343126} {:answer/id #uuid "fd324015-e5ac-5491-9cfa-1138ef253215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343016} :answer-field/id #uuid "f03d5f3b-a2cc-54d5-8a78-9ff987d46a0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "70ad4034-9f56-5cca-8517-cdacbbf852a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-76476" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9fd222d255fd1a37f00cbe7c9abf6af0d0e3af92f9ac581591bb1062780e69fa.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $ABCA,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC=\\{-{C}_{1}\\}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{1}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "908ad41e-6b90-53c7-a0b0-1379b101bcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179231} {:answer/id #uuid "cddf3bc2-f4f4-5b96-9207-d2a635343e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179232} {:answer/id #uuid "05bc89cf-d69a-58a1-8b47-152158c35292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179233} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3ec1ec-5a8b-5453-bd05-2a07d125bb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179234} {:answer/id #uuid "465dc9bc-ab17-545e-a45e-f7d0b63a0a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179235}] :answer-field/id #uuid "53b18fe0-468e-5cc2-be74-8e5b87b85f7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179230}] :question/id #uuid "5add58ef-e981-55d7-918e-05a8761bd0e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-76565" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = 2x - 3y$ along the line segment from $(1, 0)$ to $(-1, 0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37eefa34-5c7b-5c18-b8ed-71a775d5e074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348952} {:answer/id #uuid "3de4c8b4-1db8-5fc8-a86d-71a5c61c7d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349007} {:answer/id #uuid "ab99d944-7135-5a96-8ebe-a930f4912b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349969} {:answer/id #uuid "be137474-0788-5e5a-961f-c0c550ca9ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350136} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9c8ba2-744b-59f6-8a7e-28308457b79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348952} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7c6a695-a9f0-5e4f-ad87-ac5134afda03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5d8fdb2d-e684-54ed-a57b-9028bda74ea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81096" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(0,1)$ to the point $N(3,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx-y\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=3\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t(3\\,i+3\\,j-j) \\\\  & =j+t(3\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx-y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t)^{2}\\cdot 3-(1+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}27{t}^{2}-2-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[9{t}^{3}-2t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[9(1)^{3}-2(1)-2(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22633649-e91e-5f15-bfbd-fcb4a483affb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348742} {:answer/id #uuid "3f792e82-8361-5835-a14b-ecd98c471f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349024} {:answer/id #uuid "9e65a7c0-308a-5d44-8737-c54996be3dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349860} {:answer/id #uuid "bc1e3e20-823b-5b23-90e3-b832e595b63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350119} {:answer/id #uuid "f37cb0c3-6210-59b3-8ea9-2f0e5f7a13b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349024} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb520da-e760-5dc7-8812-5ec17ba0763e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e2bdab86-46e0-5604-a07d-20d0ff29dcb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81178" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=(x+1)^{2}$ traversed from the point $P(0,1)$ to point $Q(1,4),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=(x+1)^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+(t+1)^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2(t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1+2(t+1)}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{t+1}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{t+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{1+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)]-[-\\frac{1}{0+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]-[-1+0] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
